"""Component layers.1.mlp.down_proj:3440, layer 1 MLP (v7, rule-based).

What it does: this component writes a strong negative signal at the word that opens a
relative or embedded clause about manner, degree, extent or time. Examples:
  - "the way [in which / that] ..."          (the way: strongly negative)
  - "the extent to [which] ...", "in [which]", "by [which]"   (which after a preposition)
  - "the first [time] ...", "the number of [times] ...", "many [times]"
  - "how [well/fast/quickly/far/much] ..."   (how, then the degree word)
  - "whether or [not] ..."
  - "the reason [why] ...", "the time [when] ..."
Question words in plain questions ("What is ...", "How do ...", ", which") stay near zero, and so
does most text. A small positive bump appears on "same [as]".

Why: the component's job is to mark a wh/relative clause that describes HOW, TO WHAT DEGREE,
or WHEN something happens. The program is a lookup over the current word and the one or two
words before it, scaled to activation units (mean activation in each context).
